{"benchmark": "livebench_selected", "task_id": "paraphrase:f3472a74bc480a915c2a3875c505fd5b3ebe475d25cefdae9447002ed88b0106", "sample_idx": 19, "model_name": "mistralai/mistral-small-4-119b-2603", "content": "Oregon’s pioneering effort to decriminalize drugs will conclude on Sunday, replaced by a revised law that re-criminalizes possession of small quantities of substances. The incoming regulation, HB4002, offers individuals caught with drugs like fentanyl, heroin, or meth an alternative: face charges or enroll in a recovery program, including therapy and a deflection initiative, to avoid penalties. Violations would now be classified as misdemeanors, carrying potential jail time up to six months. The law also strengthens enforcement against drug sales near sensitive places such as parks, aiming to curb public drug use.\n\nCounties are encouraged, though not required, to establish treatment options to guide people away from legal consequences and toward support services. Supporters argue this flexibility allows regions to tailor programs to their means, but critics warn it could lead to uneven policies across the state. The deflection process requires strict eligibility—no additional charges, no outstanding warrants, stable health—before officers connect individuals to mobile outreach teams within half an hour.\n\nPortland’s police chief, Bob Day, emphasized the narrow criteria for deflection. Officers must quickly verify eligibility and summon a health team; if the team cannot arrive within 30 minutes, the individual will be jailed. “We can’t just delay indefinitely,” Day noted, highlighting the pressure to act swiftly under the new system.\n\nThe shift reflects a broader debate over balancing enforcement with compassionate responses to addiction.\n\n***\nP.P.S ['age', 'country', 'eat', 'shopping', 'tongue']", "logprobs": [{"token": "O", "logprob": -0.0029478687793016434}, {"token": "regon", "logprob": -2.622600959512056e-06}, {"token": "’s", "logprob": -0.147045657